{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>For everything a season.</b> Men of balanced judgment interpret these times, which are contiguous to man, as meaning that a person must do every thing in its time. The beginning of the times and their end will corrupt one interpretation of theirs: 'a time to be born and a time to die,' and the last, 'everything He has made beautiful in its time.' Some say these twenty-eight times correspond to the twenty-eight forms of the zodiac in which the moon appears each month; this too is hebel. The correct interpretation is that for every thing under the heavens there is a season and a time, for when the configuration that generates perishes, the offspring dies, and a new configuration enriches it—and the opposite of wealth, for they are fixed times. When the time arrives, a man moves toward that which is prepared for him, and his movements are like the movement of a shadow, as in 'surely man walks in a shadow' (Psalms 39:7). If so, 'surely in hebel they rage'—its meaning is: they amass wealth; like it, 'the multitude of the wicked' (Proverbs 28:28) and likewise 'who loves multitudes' (Ecclesiastes 5:8). The proof is the completion of the verse: 'he amasses and knows not who gathers it in.'"], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The phrase ואם כן אך הבל יהמיון is textually difficult; read hyper-literally as 'and if so, surely hebel they rage.' The interpretation that follows ('they amass wealth') appears to derive the meaning of יהמיון from המון in the sense of amassing, though the syntactic link is strained."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:22.238025487Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}